      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "In the past, hypersensitivity pneumonitis has been attributed to occupational, agricultural, or home environmental exposure."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "This report describes the first case of hypersensitivity pneumonitis due to community exposure to droppings from Canada geese migrating through a suburban environment."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHOD",
          "content": "Clinical and serologic information was used in making the diagnosis of hypersensitivity pneumonitis."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Serologic analysis demonstrated precipitating antibodies against goose droppings and against an extract made from washings from a filter taken from the patient's office. These studies also showed that the antigens in the office filter were goose dropping antigens."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Hypersensitivity pneumonitis can result from exposure to goose dropping antigens in the community that enter buildings through ventilation systems. This represents a new form of an old disease."
        }
      ]},
